  1060	\section{Applying the classical low-degree test}
  1061	\label{sec:apply-ldt}
  1062	
  1063	The combined point and line measurements of the previous two sections are next
  1064	merged into a single measurement returning a pair of global polynomials that
  1065	encode the $X$-basis and the $Z$-basis information simultaneously. This is
  1066	done by encoding the pair as one polynomial in $2m+2$ variables and invoking
  1067	the quantum soundness of the classical low individual degree test
  1068	(Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness}). The classical game, its question
  1069	distribution, and the line-point distribution are defined at dimension $2m+2$
  1070	only when $2m+2$ divides $q$ (Definition~\ref{def:ld-game}), a condition that
  1071	admissibility of $(q,m,d)$ does not supply; the statements below are the
  1072	source statements, without that condition, and their proofs are blocked at
  1073	the instantiation step for admissible parameters with $m \ge 2$. The
  1074	obstruction is recorded in Remark~\ref{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility} and
  1075	analyzed, together with candidate repairs, in
  1076	\cite{gap:qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility}.
