  1095		The combining map is also defined for polynomials restricted to lines.
  1096		Let $\ell$ be a line in $\F_q^{2m+2}$ with intercept
  1097		$u = (u_X, u_Z, u_\alpha, u_\beta)$ and slope
  1098		$v = (v_X, v_Z, v_\alpha, v_\beta)$, and let $\ell_X$ and $\ell_Z$ be
  1099		lines in $\F_q^m$ such that for every point
  1100		$(x, z, \alpha, \beta) \in \ell$ it holds that $x \in \ell_X$ and
  1101		$z \in \ell_Z$. Then for any two polynomials
  1102		$f \in \deg_{md}(\ell_X)$ and $g \in \deg_{md}(\ell_Z)$, the polynomial
  1103		$\mathrm{combine}_{f,g} \in \deg_{md+1}(\ell)$ is defined by
  1104		\begin{equation}
  1105			\label{eq:combine-lines}
  1106			\mathrm{combine}_{f,g}(u + t \cdot v)
  1107			= (u_\alpha + t \cdot v_\alpha)\, f(u_X + t \cdot v_X)
  1108			+ (u_\beta + t \cdot v_\beta)\, g(u_Z + t \cdot v_Z)\,,
  1109			\qquad t \in \F_q\,.
  1110		\end{equation}
